readings.c1s1:
{{smaller|cease her machinations till Troy was destroyed.—'''Apple of Sodom''', or '''Dead Sea apple.''' (''a'') A fruit said to grow on or near the site of the Biblical Sodom, described by Josephus and other old writers as externally of fair appearance, but turning to smoke and ashes when plucked. Many unsatisfactory attempts have been made to account for the tradition. (''b'') Figuratively, some fruitless thing; something which disappoints one’s hopes or frustrates one’s desires.—'''Apple of the eye.''' (''a'') The pupil.}}
{{smaller block|Dull people turn up the palms of their hands and the ''apples of their eyes'', on beholding prose by a poet.
{{right|''Blackwood’s Mag.'', XXII. 374.}}}}
{{smaller|Hence—(''b'') Something very important, precious, or dear.}}
{{smaller block|He kept him as the ''apple of his eye.'' Deut. xxxii. 10.

Poor Richard was to me as an eldest son, the ''apple of my eye.''
{{right|''Scott'', Old Mortality, xx.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Winter apple''', an apple that keeps well in winter, or does not ripen till winter.}}

'''apple''' (ap′l), ''v.'' [< AS. *''æpplian'', used only in pp. ''æppled'', appled, formed like an apple; < ''æppel'', an apple: see the noun.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To give the form of an apple to.

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To grow into the form of an apple.
{{smaller block|The cabbage turnip is of two kinds; one ''apples'' above ground, the other in it.
{{right|''C. Marshall'', Gardening.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
MHG. ''apfalter'', ''apfolter'', ''affalter'' = Icel. ''apaldr'' = Norw. dial. ''apald'', ''apall'', ''apal'', ''aple'' = Sw. ''apel'' = Dan. ''abild'', an apple-tree; a word still existing in the E. place-name ''Appledore'' (< AS. ''Apulder'', ''Apolder''). The same termination occurs in AS. ''mapulder'', ''mapuldor'', ''mapuldur'', ''mapuldern'', a maple-tree: see ''maple-tree''.] A tree (''Pyrus Malus'') bearing apples. {{smaller|Its wood is hard, durable, and fine-grained, and much used in turnery. Apple-trees are propagated by seeds, layers, grafting, and budding.}}

'''appliable†''' (a-plī′a-bl), ''a.'' [< ''apply'' + ''-able''. Cf. ''applicable''.] '''1.''' Capable of being applied; applicable; pertinent; suitable.
{{smaller block|All that I have said of . . . heathen idolatry is ''appliable'' to . . . idolatry of another sort.
{{right|''South.''}}}}
'''2.''' Willing to apply one’s self; compliant; disposed to listen.
{{smaller block|Apte by goodnes of witte, and ''appliable'' by readines of will, to learning.
{{right|''Ascham'', The Scholemaster, p. 38.}}}}

'''appliance''' (ạ-plī′ạns), ''n.'' [< ''apply'' + ''-ance''.] '''1.''' The act of applying, putting to use, or carrying into practice.
{{smaller block|The attention to fashion, the tasteful ''appliance'' of ornament in each portion of her dress, were quite in place with her.
{{right|''Charlotte Brontë'', Shirley, xvi.}}}}
'''2.''' Something applied as a means to an end, either independently or subordinately; that which is adapted to the accomplishment of a purpose; an instrumental means, aid, or appurtenance: as, the ''appliances'' of civilization, or of a trade; mechanical, chemical, or medical ''appliances'' (tools, machinery, apparatus, remedies, etc.); an engine with its ''appliances''.

readings.c2s2:
¶
{{smaller block|<poem>Diseases, desperate grown,
By desperate ''appliance'' are reliev’d.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, iv. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|Material ''appliances'' have been lavishly used; arts, inventions, and machines introduced from abroad, manufactures set up, communications opened, roads made, canals dug, mines worked, harbours formed.
{{right|''Buckle'', Civilization, I. 1.}}}}

'''appliant†''' (a-plī′ant), ''a.'' '''1.''' Favorably inclined; docile; pliant.—'''2.''' Applicable: with ''to''.

'''applicability''' (ap″li-ka-bil′i̤-ti), ''n.'' [< ''applicable'': see ''-bility''.] The quality of being applicable, or fit to be applied; capability of being applied or used; pertinence.
{{smaller block|He who has read his Aristotle will be apt to think that observation has on most points of general ''applicability'' said its last word.
{{right|''Lowell'', Democracy.}}}}
{{smaller block|Why need I speak of steam, the enemy of space and time, with its enormous strength and delicate ''applicability?''
{{right|''Emerson'', Works and Days.}}}}

'''applicable''' (ap′li-ka̤-bl), ''a.'' [= F. ''applicable'' = It. ''applicabile'', < L. ''applicare'': see ''apply'', and cf. ''appliable''.] Capable of being applied; fit to be applied; having relevance; suitable; appropriate; pertinent: as, this observation is ''applicable'' to the case under consideration.
{{smaller block|The use of logic, although potentially ''applicable'' to every matter, is always actually manifested by special reference to some one.
{{right|''Sir W. Hamilton'', Logic, iii.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|<poem>The rest [physicians] have worn me out
With several ''applications''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', All’s Well, i. 2.}}}}

'''3.''' The act of making request or of soliciting; the request so made: as, he made ''application'' to the Court of Chancery.

{{smaller block|One Sidney gave his patronage to the ''applications'' of a poet; the other offered it unasked.
{{right|''Dryden'', Ded. of Don Sebastian.}}}}

'''4.''' The act of putting to a special use or purpose; adaptation to a specific end.

{{smaller block|What we buy in a broom, a mat, a wagon, a knife, is some ''application'' of good sense to a common want.
{{right|''Emerson'', Compensation.}}}}

'''5.''' The act of fixing the mind on something; close attention; devotion, as to a pursuit; assiduous effort.

{{smaller block|The curate, surprised to find such instances of industry and ''application'' in a young man who had never met with the least encouragement, asked him if he did not extremely regret the want of a liberal education.
{{right|''Fielding'', Joseph Andrews.}}}}

'''6.''' The act of applying a general principle, law, or theory to a particular case; the demonstration of the relation of a general principle to an actual state of things; the testing of something theoretical by applying it in practice.

{{smaller block|He laid down with clearness and accuracy the principles by which the question is to be decided, but he did not pursue them into their detailed ''application''.
{{right|''Sir G. C. Lewis'', Cred. of Early Roman Hist., I. 5.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller block|Faith is the inward ''applicatory'' [of Christ’s death], and if there be any outward, it must be the sacraments.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Worthy Communicant, i. § 4.}}}}

'''applied''' (a̤-plīd′), ''p. a.'' [< ''apply'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Put on; put to; directed; employed: said specifically of a science when its laws are employed and exemplified in dealing with concrete phenomena, and in this use distinguished from ''abstract'' or ''theoretical''.{{smaller|—'''Applied chemistry, logic, mathematics''', etc. See the nouns.—'''Applied work.''' See ''appliqué''.}}

'''appliedly''' (a̤-plī′ed-li), ''adv.'' By or in application. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|All superstition whatsoever reflecteth upon religion. It is not but in such acts as be of themselves, or ''appliedly'', acts of religion and piety.
{{right|''Bp. Mountagu'', Appeal to Cæsar, p. 267.}}}}

'''applier''' (a̤-plī′ėr), ''n.'' '''1.''' One who applies.—'''2.''' A dental instrument for placing a piece of floss-silk between teeth.

readings.c3s4:
¶
'''appliment†''', ''n.'' Same as ''applyment.''

'''appliqué''' (ap-li-kā′), ''a.'' [< F. ''appliqué'', pp. of ''appliquer'', put on, < L. ''applicare'': see ''apply''.] '''1.''' In modern dress and upholstery, applied or sewed on, or produced in this way. {{smaller|Thus, the gimp or pattern of soiled or injured lace may be sewed upon a new ground, or embroidered flowers may be secured to new silk; in such a case the pattern or ornament is said to be ''appliqué'', and the whole is ''appliqué work.''}} '''2.''' More generally, said of one material, as metal, fixed upon another, in ornamental work: as, an enameled disk ''appliqué'' upon a surface of filigree, an ivory figure ''appliqué'' upon a Japanese lacquer, and the like. [In both senses also used as a noun.]—'''Point appliqué''', {{smaller|point-lace in which the design, after having been separately made, has been applied to the net which forms the foundation.}}
